"""Chapter 4, first half: architecture, security, field mapping, crop ranking, irrigation."""

BLOCKS = [
    ("h1", "CHAPTER – 4: PROPOSED SOLUTION"),
    ("h2", "4.1 System overview and architecture"),
    ("p", "Dhartisense is a three-part system, shown in {fig:arch}. The browser holds the whole interface as plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no build step. A single Python process, written against the standard-library HTTP server, serves those files and a JSON interface, enforces sessions and rate limits, and runs the engines. Firebase provides accounts and storage. Everything else is a free public service that the server or the browser calls directly."),
    ("fig", "arch", "figures/diagrams/architecture.png", "Architecture of Dhartisense: browser, Python server with its five engines, Firebase, and the external services and data files the engines depend on.", 6.4),
    ("p", "The server has no web framework. It handles routes in one handler class, which keeps the dependency list to two packages (`firebase-admin` and Pillow) plus PyTorch when the local plant model is used [@pytorch]. The engines are plain Python modules with no network access of their own. The server fetches weather, passes the numbers in, and receives a dictionary back. This split lets each engine be tested with invented inputs and kept free of side effects. {tab:stack} lists the technologies."),
    ("tab", "stack", "Technology stack",
     ["Layer", "Choice", "Role"],
     [["Server", "Python 3.12, standard-library HTTP server", "Routes, sessions, rate limits, engine calls"],
      ["Browser app", "HTML, CSS, JavaScript; Leaflet [@leaflet]; Lucide icons", "Interface, map drawing, charts, calendar export"],
      ["Accounts and data", "Firebase Authentication, Cloud Firestore [@firebase]", "Sign-in, email verification, saved fields"],
      ["Weather", "Open-Meteo forecast and archive [@openmeteo]", "Temperature, rain and FAO ET₀, past and forecast"],
      ["Satellite", "Sentinel-2 L2A via Planetary Computer [@pc]", "NDVI statistics and tiles"],
      ["Imagery and search", "Esri imagery, OpenStreetMap Nominatim and Photon [@nominatim]", "Map layers, place search"],
      ["Plant model", "PyTorch, torchvision, ConvNeXt-Tiny [@pytorch]", "Local leaf classifier"],
      ["Language models", "Groq API [@groq]", "Assistant, photo second opinion"]],
     [1700, 4200, 4267]),
    ("h2", "4.2 Accounts, sessions and data storage"),
    ("p", "Sign-up and sign-in use Firebase Authentication with email and password. Firebase stores the passwords, sends the verification and reset emails, and throttles abuse, so the application never handles a password. Before sign-up the server checks that the email is well formed and that its domain publishes a mail server, using only the domain in a public DNS-over-HTTPS query. If that lookup itself fails, the address is accepted, since the verification email is the real proof."),
    ("p", "After a sign-in the browser holds a short-lived Firebase ID token. It sends the token to the server, which verifies it and refuses the request unless the email is verified and the sign-in happened within the last hour. The server then exchanges the token for a Firebase session cookie that is HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax and valid for 14 days, and sets the Secure flag when the site runs over HTTPS. {fig:session} shows the sequence."),
    ("fig", "session", "figures/diagrams/session.png", "Sign-in and session sequence between the browser, Firebase Authentication, the Dhartisense server and Firestore.", 6.2),
    ("p", "Fields are stored in Firestore at `users/{uid}/fields/{id}`. Only the server writes there, through the Admin SDK. The published Firestore rules deny every read and write from a browser, so a leaked web configuration cannot expose data. Each field record is a JSON document limited to 300,000 bytes, a farmer can keep at most 50 fields, and a field identifier must match a strict pattern before it is used in a path, which blocks identifiers crafted to reach another user's documents. The self-check of this layer confirms that a second user cannot update or delete the first user's field."),
    ("p", "The server adds several further controls, summarised in {tab:security}. Per-user rate limits apply to the routes that spend a free quota. Every response carries a content security policy that allows scripts only from the application itself and one pinned CDN, forbids framing and objects, and limits network connections to the named services. Photos arrive as base64 text capped at 12 MB per request and are refused above 25 million pixels, which prevents decompression bombs from filling memory."),
    ("tab", "security", "Security controls in the server",
     ["Control", "Setting"],
     [["Session cookie", "HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, 14 days; Secure and HSTS when served over HTTPS"],
      ["Origin check", "Write requests must come from the site's own origin (or an allowed list)"],
      ["Public routes", "Health, configuration and sign-in only; every other route needs a session"],
      ["Rate limits per user per minute", "Assistant 20, photo scan 10, photo advice 20, NDVI 6, crop ranking 12, water plan 20, place search 30, weather 60"],
      ["Content security policy", "default-src self; scripts from self and one CDN; frame-ancestors none; object-src none; named connect targets"],
      ["Other headers", "X-Content-Type-Options nosniff, X-Frame-Options DENY, Referrer-Policy same-origin, restrictive Permissions-Policy"],
      ["Input limits", "Request body 12 MB; photo 25 million pixels; field record 300,000 bytes; 50 fields; boundary 3 to 300 points on the satellite route"],
      ["Storage rules", "Firestore denies all direct browser access; only the server's Admin SDK reads and writes"]],
     [2700, 7467]),
    ("h2", "4.3 Field mapping and geometry"),
    ("p", "The map uses Leaflet with three free layers: Esri Clarity imagery by default, classic Esri imagery, and a place-name overlay. Place search queries Nominatim and Photon, limited to India first and then worldwide, and accepts raw coordinates such as `18.52, 73.85`. The farmer traces the boundary by tapping corners, or switches to freehand drawing for curved edges. A freehand stroke is simplified with a tolerance that starts at 1.5 m and grows by half until at most 120 corners remain, so a long trace stays editable."),
    ("p", "Area, perimeter, length, width and direction are computed in the browser as the boundary changes. Coordinates are converted to metres on a local plane using the mean latitude φ₀ of the boundary. With λ the longitude and φ the latitude of each corner, the planar coordinates are x = λ·111320·cos φ₀ and y = φ·110574, and the area follows from the shoelace formula:"),
    ("eq", "area", r"A=\frac{1}{2}\left|\sum_{i=1}^{n}{\left(x_i y_{i+1}-x_{i+1} y_i\right)}\right|"),
    ("p", "The approximation is adequate for field-sized shapes, where the curvature of the Earth is negligible. For length, width and direction, the code rotates the boundary through 180 one-degree steps and keeps the orientation whose bounding rectangle has the smallest area. The longer side of that rectangle is the field's length and its direction gives the row direction. The same formula is repeated on the server in `app/ndvi.py` to refuse fields below 1,000 m², and the field-plan layout reuses the local frame to place crop rows."),
    ("h2", "4.4 Crop ranking engine"),
    ("p", "The ranking answers one question: for this field and planting date, which crops fit best and are also grown nearby? It makes no yield forecast. The pipeline is shown in {fig:croppipe}."),
    ("fig", "croppipe", "figures/diagrams/crop_pipeline.png", "Crop ranking pipeline from the saved field and planting date to the five suggested crops.", 6.4),
    ("h3", "4.4.1 Data"),
    ("p", "Two derived files feed the engine. The first, `crop_requirements.json`, holds the EcoCrop ranges of 38 field crops: absolute minimum, optimal minimum, optimal maximum and absolute maximum for temperature, rainfall and pH, the preferred and tolerated soil texture classes, and the length of the growing cycle. A build script reads the FAO database and copies every number as published; only the display names are the project's own. Crops whose EcoCrop row was clearly unsuitable for Indian farming, or had no season length, were left out on purpose."),
    ("p", "The second, `regional_crops.json`, is built from the district statistics [@districts]. For each district and season (Kharif from June to September, Rabi from October to January, and Summer from February to May) it stores each ranked crop's share of the cropped area and its typical yield in tonnes per hectare, averaged over 2008 to 2015. The data cover 484 districts in 27 states; 463 have Kharif data, 447 Rabi and 341 Summer. District centres were placed with the Open-Meteo geocoder, and a district that could not be placed was dropped. Cotton, reported in bales, is converted to tonnes at 170 kg per bale. The rice seasons named Autumn and Winter in eastern India are treated as Kharif, and crops that grow all year (sugarcane, turmeric and ginger) count in every season."),
    ("h3", "4.4.2 Fit to the field"),
    ("p", "For each crop and each past year with a complete record, the engine takes the window that starts on the planting date and lasts as long as the crop cycle. The cycle is the sum of the FAO-56 stage lengths when the crop has them, and otherwise a quarter of the way up EcoCrop's range of cycle lengths. A crop needs at least three complete past seasons to be scored."),
    ("p", "Every requirement is a four-point range (x₁, x₂, x₃, x₄): absolute minimum, optimal minimum, optimal maximum and absolute maximum. The trapezoid function scores a value as 1 inside the optimal band and lets it fall linearly to 0 at the absolute limits:"),
    ("eq", "trap", r"T(x)=\cases{0, \text{ if } x\le x_1 \text{ or } x\ge x_4 @@ \frac{x-x_1}{x_2-x_1}, \text{ if } x_1<x<x_2 @@ 1, \text{ if } x_2\le x\le x_3 @@ \frac{x_4-x}{x_4-x_3}, \text{ if } x_3<x<x_4}"),
    ("p", "Several crops can score 1 for the same field, so a small term separates them by how near the value lies to the middle of the optimal band. With m = (x₂ + x₃)/2, the centred fit is"),
    ("eq", "centred", r"C(x)=T(x)\left[0.8+0.2\max\left(0,\;1-\frac{\left|x-m\right|}{\left(x_4-x_1\right)/2}\right)\right]".replace(r"\;", "")),
    ("p", "so a value at the centre of the band keeps its full score and a value at the edge of the band keeps 80% of it. {fig:c_tfit} plots both functions for the temperature range of one crop. Temperature is scored every day of the season window and averaged over the days and the years, with Y past years and L days per season:"),
    ("eq", "tfit", r"F_T=\frac{1}{Y}\sum_{y=1}^{Y}{\frac{1}{L}\sum_{d=1}^{L}{C_T\left(T_{y,d}\right)}}"),
    ("colab", "c_tfit", "Trapezoid and centred fit for the daily mean temperature of one crop, drawn from the EcoCrop values stored in the project.", "plot of T(x) and C(x) against temperature for one crop", 5.2),
    ("p", "Rainfall is scored on the seasonal total averaged over the past years. When the farmer says the field is irrigated, the engine assumes that irrigation makes up any shortage but never removes an excess, so rainfall is scored at the larger of the real total and the crop's minimum. Soil pH, when the farmer gives it, uses the same centred function. Soil texture is mapped to the EcoCrop classes (sand to light; loam, silt loam and clay loam to medium; clay to heavy) and scores 1.0 if the class is the crop's preferred one, 0.75 if it is tolerated and 0.3 otherwise. Crops marked as suited to a wide range of textures, or fields without texture, give no texture score."),
    ("p", "The factors are combined with a weighted geometric mean, with weights 2 for temperature, 2 for rainfall, and 1 each for pH and texture. A geometric mean lets one failed factor pull the whole score down, which suits limiting conditions: a perfect temperature does not rescue a crop for which pH is impossible. If any factor is exactly zero the crop scores zero."),
    ("eq", "score", r"S=\cases{0, \text{ if some } F_k=0 @@ \exp\left(\frac{\sum_{k}{w_k\ln F_k}}{\sum_{k}{w_k}}\right), \text{ otherwise}}"),
    ("h3", "4.4.3 What farmers nearby grow"),
    ("p", "A fit score says what a crop can tolerate. To add what farmers choose, the engine looks up the five districts nearest to the field that have data for the planting season, and ignores them if the nearest is more than 400 km away, as happens outside India. Distance is the great-circle distance from the haversine formula, with R the Earth's radius and φ and λ latitude and longitude:"),
    ("eq", "hav", r"d=2R\arcsin\sqrt{\sin^{2}\left(\frac{\varphi_2-\varphi_1}{2}\right)+\cos\varphi_1\cos\varphi_2\sin^{2}\left(\frac{\lambda_2-\lambda_1}{2}\right)}"),
    ("p", "Closer districts count more. Each district k receives the weight w_{k} below, with d_{k} in kilometres, where the constant 30 stops a district at almost zero distance from taking all the weight. The share s_{c} of crop c is the weighted mean of the district shares s_{c,k}:"),
    ("eq", "weight", r"w_k=\frac{1}{d_k+30}"),
    ("eq", "share", r"s_c=\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{5}{w_k\,s_{c,k}}}{\sum_{k=1}^{5}{w_k}}".replace(r"\,", " ")),
    ("p", "The typical yield of the crop is averaged with the same weights, using only districts where the crop is grown. The final score blends local practice with fit. Let r_{c} = s_{c} divided by the largest share among all crops, S_{c} the fit score on a scale of 0 to 100, and φ the blending constant FIT_FLOOR, set to 0.5:"),
    ("eq", "blend", r"P_c=100\left(0.05+0.95\,r_c\right)\left(\phi+\left(1-\phi\right)\frac{S_c}{100}\right)".replace(r"\,", " ")),
    ("p", "With φ = 0.5, a crop that farmers nearby grow on the most land loses half of its score when it does not fit the field at all, and keeps all of it when it fits perfectly. The value 0.5 is a design setting, and Section 5.3 reports how the benchmark hit rate changes as φ varies. Two rules keep common crops from outranking the weather: if a crop's temperature factor is below 0.1, or the field is rain-fed and the season's rain is under 30% of the crop's minimum, the blended score cannot exceed the fit score."),
    ("h3", "4.4.4 Output"),
    ("p", "Crops below a score of 10 are never offered. The best five are shown one card at a time, each with a label (Good match from 75, Fair match from 55, otherwise Weak match), the four factor scores, the plain-language reasons, the length of the season, the nearby share and typical yield, and the irrigation need. That need comes from the water engine of the next section, not from EcoCrop's rain range. The farmer can pick the crops to be grown and the picks appear on the Overview page. Three ordering objectives are offered: best overall fit, lowest risk (largest weakest factor first) and lowest water need. If the planting date is within a week and the forecast mean temperature lies outside the crop's tolerated range, the card adds a note advising the farmer to wait."),
    ("h2", "4.5 Irrigation scheduling engine"),
    ("p", "The irrigation engine implements the FAO-56 daily soil-water balance for one crop on one field [@fao56]. {fig:waterloop} shows the daily loop and the points where the farmer's information enters."),
    ("fig", "waterloop", "figures/diagrams/water_loop.png", "Daily soil-water balance used by the irrigation engine, with the decision logic that produces a schedule.", 6.4),
    ("h3", "4.5.1 Crop water use and the root zone"),
    ("p", "The weather service supplies daily FAO reference evapotranspiration ET₀ and rain, for the last 92 days and the next 16 days. Crop water use is the product of ET₀ and a crop coefficient that depends on growth stage:"),
    ("eq", "etc", r"\text{ET}_{c}=K_{c}\cdot\text{ET}_{0}"),
    ("p", "Each crop has an FAO-56 record: coefficients for the initial, mid-season and end stages (K_{ini}, K_{mid}, K_{end}), the lengths in days of four stages (a, b, c and d), the maximum rooting depth and the depletion fraction p. Of the 38 crops, 26 take every number from FAO-56 tables or a stated row, and 12 borrow a related crop's values or estimate stage lengths; the farmer sees those marked as approximate. The coefficient follows the stage by linear interpolation, where t is the number of days after planting:"),
    ("eq", "kc", r"K_c(t)=\cases{K_{\text{ini}}, \text{ if } t<a @@ K_{\text{ini}}+\left(K_{\text{mid}}-K_{\text{ini}}\right)\frac{t-a}{b}, \text{ if } a\le t<a+b @@ K_{\text{mid}}, \text{ if } a+b\le t<a+b+c @@ K_{\text{mid}}+\left(K_{\text{end}}-K_{\text{mid}}\right)\min\left(1,\frac{t-a-b-c}{d}\right), \text{ otherwise}}"),
    ("p", "The root zone deepens as the crop grows. The depth grows linearly from 0.25 m at planting to the crop's maximum Z_{max} at the end of the vegetative stage:"),
    ("eq", "root", r"Z_r(t)=\min\left(Z_{\text{max}},\;0.25+\left(Z_{\text{max}}-0.25\right)\min\left(1,\frac{t}{a+b}\right)\right)".replace(r"\;", "")),
    ("colab", "c_kc", "Crop coefficient and root depth through the season for three crops, from the stage tables of the irrigation engine.", "curves of Kc and root depth against days after planting", 5.6),
    ("p", "The water the root zone can hold between field capacity and wilting point is the total available water, TAW. It is the product of the soil's water-holding capacity AWC (in mm per metre) and the root depth. Typical AWC values by texture are 80 for sand, 140 for loam, 170 for silt loam, 160 for clay loam and 150 for clay, and 140 is used when the texture is unknown. These are general soil-physics values, not a measurement of the farmer's soil, and the application says so. Stress begins once a share p of the TAW is gone. That readily available water, RAW, is also capped by the largest depth one irrigation can usefully apply, which depends on the method (drip 25 mm, sprinkler 50, furrow and flood 75, otherwise 60):"),
    ("eq", "taw", r"\text{TAW}=\text{AWC}\cdot Z_r"),
    ("eq", "raw", r"\text{RAW}=\min\left(p\cdot\text{TAW},\;\text{cap}\right)".replace(r"\;", "")),
    ("h3", "4.5.2 Daily balance and the irrigation decision"),
    ("p", "The engine keeps a depletion D, the water missing from the root zone, and updates it each day. Showers under 3 mm are assumed to wet the surface and evaporate, and 80% of larger amounts counts as effective rain:"),
    ("eq", "dep", r"D_t=\max\left(0,\;D_{t-1}+\text{ET}_{c,t}-P_{e,t}\right)".replace(r"\;", "")),
    ("eq", "peff", r"P_{e}=\cases{0.8\,P, \text{ if } P\ge 3\text{ mm} @@ 0, \text{ otherwise}}".replace(r"\,", " ")),
    ("p", "Irrigation is due on the first day when D reaches RAW. The depth to apply is D, the net requirement, and the depth to pump is the gross requirement after losses, where E_{a} is the application efficiency of the method (drip 0.90, sprinkler 0.75, furrow 0.60, flood 0.55, and 0.65 when the method is unknown) [@savva]:"),
    ("eq", "gross", r"V_{\text{gross}}=\frac{D}{E_a}"),
    ("p", "The engine then applies the rules that make the result usable in practice. When irrigation is due today but the next three days of forecast rain would supply at least 60% of the missing water, the status is set to wait for rain instead of recommending irrigation. When the farmer gives a last irrigation date, the balance is restarted from that date with nothing assumed after it; otherwise the engine assumes the farmer irrigated whenever the crop would have been stressed. A statement about the soil surface today (dry and dusty, dry below the surface, moist, wet or standing water) sets the depletion to 100%, 80%, 40%, 15% or 0% of RAW, because what the farmer sees outranks the model. If the farmer irrigated within five days but reports dry soil, the engine adds a note to check that the water reached the whole field."),
    ("colab", "c_water", "Simulated root-zone depletion and irrigation rounds for wheat over one season, using real past weather at a sample location.", "depletion curve against RAW with irrigation events and rain bars", 5.8),
    ("p", "Forecast days, run without irrigation, show the first day that the crop would run short. The result is one of six statuses: before sowing, season over, irrigate now, irrigate soon with a date, wait for rain, or no irrigation needed in the next 14 days. Dates beyond the forecast horizon come from an outlook: the engine replays the same calendar dates in each of the past years that have a full record, lets a virtual farmer irrigate whenever the crop would be stressed, and reports the median day and depth of each irrigation round. The same outlook serves fields that have not been sown yet and supplies the irrigation figure on the crop cards. The farmer can add the next dates to Google Calendar or download an `.ics` file with a reminder the evening before and on the morning of each date."),
]
